  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+4$ and $L({y}_{2})=3(x-2)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$",
  "worked_solution": "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-3(x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3{x}^{2}+12x-12 \\\\  & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
